[task-01] Before an injected edit's result is allowed to count as evidence, the review
    checks that the edit really makes its named defect true. Today that check is one
    model call that is asked, in the same answer, what the code does before the edit and
    what it does after it. It refuses too few bad edits and too many good ones, and many
    of its wrong refusals answer "cannot tell" rather than deciding.
    -> evidence-check-validates-named-defect  [functional/explicit]
       The review checks that an injected edit's result really makes its named defect
       true before counting it as evidence.
    -> single-call-does-not-combine-pre-and-post-judgment  [regression/inferred]
       The review's evidence check is not a single model call that asks, in one answer,
       what the code does before the edit and what it does after it.
    -> reduce-cannot-tell-refusals  [functional/inferred]
       The review's evidence check avoids wrong refusals that answer "cannot tell"
       instead of deciding.
    -> single-model-call-before-and-after  [functional/explicit]
       The review uses one model call that, in the same answer, states what the code
       does before the edit and what it does after it.
    -> refuses-too-few-bad-edits  [functional/explicit]
       The review refuses too few bad edits.
    -> too-many-good-ones  [functional/explicit]
       The review check refuses too many good edits.
    -> cannot-tell-refusals  [functional/explicit]
       The review check does not answer "cannot tell" when it can decide whether the
       edit makes its named defect true.
    -> avoid-cannot-tell-refusals  [invariant/explicit]
       The review must decide rather than answering "cannot tell" for this case.

[task-02] Split the check into two questions asked in sequence.
    -> split-check-into-two-questions  [functional/explicit]
       Split the check into two questions asked in sequence.
    -> asked-in-sequence  [functional/explicit]
       The check is split into two questions asked in sequence.

[task-03] The first asks whether the edit changes the code's behaviour at all. Both
    versions of the code are compared with comments and whitespace removed, so an edit
    that only reformats or re-comments the code is not mistaken for a change. An edit
    that changes no behaviour is refused at this step, without the defect being
    considered.
    -> behavior-change-check  [functional/explicit]
       Determine whether the edit changes the code's behaviour at all.
    -> ignore-comments-and-whitespace-in-comparison  [invariant/explicit]
       Compare both versions of the code with comments and whitespace removed.
    -> reformatting-not-counted-as-change  [invariant/explicit]
       An edit that only reformats or re-comments the code is not mistaken for a change.
    -> no-behavior-change-refusal  [invariant/explicit]
       The step refuses edits that change no behaviour, without considering the defect.

[task-04] The second is asked only about an edit that does change behaviour: does the
    changed behaviour match the defect's defective behaviour?
    -> asked-only-about-edit-that-does-change-behaviour  [invariant/explicit]
       The requirement applies only when the edit does change behaviour.
    -> defective-behaviour-match  [functional/explicit]
       The changed behaviour matches the defect's defective behaviour.

[task-05] An edit is verified only when it passes both. When an edit is refused, the
    review records which of the two questions refused it and why, so that the two can be
    told apart wherever refusals are counted.
    -> passes-both-questions  [invariant/explicit]
       An edit is verified only when it passes both questions.
    -> refusal-records-question-and-reason  [functional/explicit]
       When an edit is refused, the review records which of the two questions refused it
       and why.

[task-06] Verification is on by default.
    -> verification-on-by-default  [invariant/explicit]
       Verification is on by default.

